```
x = if (True # comment
) True else False

main! = |_| Ok({})
```

With latest nightly release (11-07) & in Linux/Windows, if you call fmt on this snippet, it will add extra parentheses around the `True # comment` stmt.
Eventually it will look like this:
```
x = if (
	(
		(
			(
				(
					(
						(
							(
								(
									(
										(
											(
												True # comment
											)
										)
									)
								)
							)
						)
					)
				)
			)
		)
	)
) True else False

main! = |_| Ok({})
```

Nightly: `nightly-2026-11-07-ad3a5d2` and `nightly-2026-11-06-34f137a` (numbers below), and `nightly-2026-10-19-601b54c` (frame of `apply` with N = 256: 152,576 B; this nightly does not export `__stack_low`, so `run.mjs` below needs 11-06 or later). Linux x86_64, `--target=wasm32 --opt=dev`.

## Summary

When a function calls a closure whose lambda set has N members, the wasm32 dev backend expands the call into one branch for each member. Each branch gets its own stack slots for the argument, the captures and the result, and the slots are not shared between branches. So the stack frame of the calling function is O(N x size of the values). In a recursion that goes through such a call, each level uses the full frame, and a 1 MB stack overflows after a few levels. The trap is `RuntimeError: memory access out of bounds` (the stack is first in memory and goes below 0).

| N closures | frame of `apply` (dev) | stack for each level (dev) | stack for each level (`--opt=speed`) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 8 | 2,624 B | 3.1 KB | |
| 64 | 18,752 B | 19.2 KB | |
| 256 | 74,048 B | 74.5 KB | 17.4 KB |

With the argument bound to a name before the call (`v = { ..w, a: w.a + 1 }` then `f(v)`), the frame is smaller (N = 256: 31,184 B) but it still grows with N, about 120 B for each member (the result slots).

A real case: the derived encoders of a CBOR package (roc-cbor 0.5.0). The writer calls the `write_value` closure of the protocol in one function, and its lambda set has a member for each item encoder of the app. In an app with many routes the frame of that one function was 336,592 B, so `{ r : { x : List(U8) } }` (3 levels) overflowed the 1 MB stack of the platform. `--opt=speed`, `--opt=size` and native dev are correct.

## Repro

`gen.py` writes `app.roc` with N closures in one lambda set:

```python
import sys
n = int(sys.argv[1])
arms = "\n".join(f"\t\t{k} => |w| go(n - 1, {{ ..w, out: w.out.append({k % 256}), b: w.b + {k} }})" for k in range(n - 1))
open("app.roc", "w").write(f'''app [main!] {{ pf: platform "platform/main.roc" }}

W : {{ out : List(U8), a : U64, b : U64, c : U64, d : U64, e : U64 }}

apply : W, (W -> W) -> W
apply = |w, f| {{
	r = f({{ ..w, a: w.a + 1 }})
	{{ ..r, d: r.d + 1 }}
}}

pick : U64, U64 -> (W -> W)
pick = |k, n|
	match k {{
{arms}
		_ => |w| go(n - 1, {{ ..w, out: w.out.append(1), b: w.b + {n} }})
	}}

go : U64, W -> W
go = |n, w|
	if (n == 0) w else {{
		r = apply(w, pick(n % {n}, n))
		{{ ..r, c: r.c + 1 }}
	}}

main! : U64 => U64
main! = |depth| go(depth, {{ out: [], a: 0, b: 0, c: 0, d: 0, e: 0 }}).out.len()
''')
```

`platform/main.roc` (no host code: the module imports the allocator from JS):

```roc
platform ""
	requires {
		main! : U64 => U64
	}
	exposes []
	packages {}
	provides { "roc_main": main_for_host! }
	targets: {
		inputs_dir: "targets/",
		wasm32: {
			inputs: ["host.wasm", app],
			output: Shared,
			exports: ["roc_main"],
		},
	}

main_for_host! : U64 => U64
main_for_host! = |seed| main!(seed)
```

`platform/targets/wasm32/host.wasm` is an empty relocatable object (19 bytes):

```sh
mkdir -p platform/targets/wasm32
printf '\0asm\1\0\0\0\0\011\007linking\002' > platform/targets/wasm32/host.wasm
```

`run.mjs` (node: a bump allocator, and the stack high-water mark from `__stack_low` and `__stack_high`):

```js
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
const [file, depth] = process.argv.slice(2);
let mem, next = 0;
const alloc = (len, align) => {
  if (next === 0) next = mem.buffer.byteLength;
  const at = Math.ceil(next / 8) * 8 + 8;
  next = at + len;
  if (next > mem.buffer.byteLength) mem.grow(Math.ceil((next - mem.buffer.byteLength) / 65536));
  new DataView(mem.buffer).setUint32(at - 4, len, true);
  return at;
};
const env = {
  roc_alloc: alloc, roc_dealloc: () => {},
  roc_realloc: (p, len, a) => { const q = alloc(len, a); new Uint8Array(mem.buffer).copyWithin(q, p, p + Math.min(new DataView(mem.buffer).getUint32(p - 4, true), len)); return q; },
  roc_crashed: () => { throw new Error('crashed'); },
};
const x = new WebAssembly.Instance(new WebAssembly.Module(readFileSync(file)), { env }).exports;
mem = x.memory;
const lo = x.__stack_low.value, hi = x.__stack_high.value;
new Uint8Array(mem.buffer).fill(0xa5, lo, hi);
let r;
try { r = x.roc_main(BigInt(depth)); } catch (e) { r = e.message; }
let low = lo;
while (low < hi && new Uint8Array(mem.buffer)[low] === 0xa5) low++;
console.log(`depth ${depth}: ${r}, stack used ${hi - low} of ${hi - lo}`);
```

Run:

```sh
python3 gen.py 256
roc build --target=wasm32 --opt=dev --wasm-stack-size=1048576 --output=app.wasm app.roc
node run.mjs app.wasm 10
node run.mjs app.wasm 20
```

## Expected

The frame of `apply` does not depend much on N (one argument, one result and one capture slot, shared by all branches). Depth 20 runs.

## Actual

```
depth 10: 10, stack used 746256 of 1048576
depth 20: memory access out of bounds, stack used 1044432 of 1048576
```

The prologue of `apply` is `global.get $__stack_pointer; i32.const 74048; i32.sub`. With `python3 gen.py 8` the constant is 2624, with 64 it is 18752. In the text of the function, each member branch writes to its own range of stack offsets (for example 29712 to 30456 in one branch, 30536 onwards in the next).

## Workaround

Bind the argument to a name before the call, and put the call alone in a small function, so that only the call is in the branches. In roc-cbor this cut the frame on the recursion path from 336,592 B to 96,496 B + 1,680 B. It is still O(N).

When compile-time evaluation hits a failed pattern match under `--specialize=no`, the report is a generic "compile time crash: hit a runtime error" instead of the "non exhaustive destructure" (or match) report that the default `--specialize=yes` path gives for the same program.

```roc
g = |_| {
	Ok(y) = Err({})
	y
}

main! = |_args| {
	echo!(Str.inspect(g({})))
	Ok({})
}
```

`roc check --specialize=yes` on this (with the echo platform, `test/echo/destructure_failure_in_called_function.roc` on the `sealed-store-switchover` branch / #12880) reports "non exhaustive destructure" at `Ok(y) = Err({})`. `roc check --specialize=no` reports the failure at the same location, but as "compile time crash: hit a runtime error".

The cause is that Boxy lowering never records compile-time exhaustiveness sites, so a destructure or match failure that compile-time evaluation reaches through Boxy-lowered code is just a crash with no site to attach the exhaustiveness report to. The `--specialize=no` CLI test for that program currently only checks the location and that nothing panics, not the report kind; it should check for "non exhaustive destructure" once this is fixed.

