A platform's provided function can take a type the application chooses, by value:

```roc
platform "glue-unresolved-arg-by-value"
    requires {
        [Model : model] for program : {
            render : model -> Str,
        }
    }
    exposes []
    packages {}
    provides {
        "roc_render": render_for_host,
    }
    targets: {}

render_for_host : Model -> Str
render_for_host = |model| (program.render)(model)
```

`roc glue --no-cache src/glue/src/ZigGlue.roc out main.roc` succeeds and declares the argument as a pointer:

```zig
pub extern fn roc_render(arg0: *anyopaque) callconv(.c) RocStr;
```

The compiled entrypoint takes the value by value under the C ABI instead. With an app whose `Model` is a 32-byte tag union, the x86_64 entry takes the return slot in `rdi` and loads the union from the caller's stack (the first in-memory argument). A host following the binding passes a pointer in `rsi`, and the function reads whatever is on the stack.

The same type variable held by value in a returned record is already refused, by `test/glue/unresolved-by-value` (#9824):

> The type `{ tag : I32, value : rigid }` from module `main` (in the signature of `init_for_host`) still has an unresolved type variable, so it has no committed memory layout and glue cannot generate bindings for it. Give the type a concrete layout (for example, box the value across the host boundary) so its size is known.

I'd expect an argument to get the same error, since the host is compiled before any app and can't know the value's size. `Box(Model)` works: glue declares it as `RocBox`, and it is what I use.

